Let $G$ be a connected simply connected real-triangulable unimodular Lie group, with a fixed left invariant Riemannian metric, exponential height map $\pi:G\to W$, and a fixed norm on $W$. There is a finite subgroup $\mathcal A_G\leq\mathop{\mathrm{GL}}(W)$ such that, for every $K\geq1$ and $C\geq0$, there is a constant $B=B(G,K,C)$ with the following property. Every $(K,C)$ self quasi-isometry $F$ of $G$ has an $A_F\in\mathcal A_G$ satisfying $$\label{eq:height-main}
 \bigl\|\pi F(g)-\pi F(1)-A_F\pi(g)\bigr\|\leq B
 \qquad(g\in G).$$

There is a real generalized weight decomposition $$\label{eq:cartan-weight-decomposition}
 \mathfrak g=\bigoplus_\gamma\mathfrak g_\gamma,
 \qquad \mathfrak g_0=\mathfrak d,
 \qquad
 [\mathfrak g_\gamma,\mathfrak g_\eta]
       \subseteq\mathfrak g_{\gamma+\eta}.$$ Let $\mathfrak n$ be the subalgebra generated by the spaces with nonzero weight. Then $\mathfrak n$ is a nilpotent ideal contained in $[\mathfrak g,\mathfrak g]$, and $\mathfrak g=\mathfrak n+\mathfrak d$. There are homogeneous generators $X_1,\ldots,X_\nu$ of $\mathfrak n$ whose images form a basis of $\mathfrak n/[\mathfrak n,\mathfrak n]$ and whose weights $\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_\nu$ are all nonzero.*


*The groups $N=\exp_G(\mathfrak n)$ and $D=\exp_G(\mathfrak d)$ are closed and simply connected, and $N$ is normal in $G$. With the conjugation action of $D$ on $N$, multiplication defines a surjective Lie homomorphism and submersion $$\label{eq:cartan-multiplication}
 q:N\rtimes D\longrightarrow G,\qquad q(n,d)=nd.$$ Its kernel is $$\label{eq:cartan-overlap-kernel}
 \ker q=\{(a^{-1},a):a\in N\cap D\}.$$ Moreover, $N\cap D=\exp_G(\mathfrak n\cap\mathfrak d)$. Every compact subset of $G$ has lifts in a compact subset of $N\rtimes D$. Finally, $N=1$ if and only if $G$ is nilpotent.

There is a constant $C\ge1$, depending only on $D$, the metric, and the splitting, such that for $d=\exp_D(\sum_jv_j)$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \|v_j\|&\le C(1+|d|_D)^j \quad(1\le j\le s),
       \label{eq:d-coordinate-upper}\\
 |d|_D&\le C\left(1+\max_{1\le j\le s}\|v_j\|^{1/j}\right).
       \label{eq:d-coordinate-reverse}
\end{aligned}$$ The group laws transported from $D$ by $\Delta_r$ converge, with all derivatives on compact coordinate sets, to the law of $D_\infty$ as $r\to\infty$. Every compact $K\subset D_\infty$ satisfies $\sup_{z\in K}|\Delta_r z|_D=O_K(r)$ for $r\ge1$. Conversely, for every fixed $C_0$, all $\Delta_r^{-1}\{d:|d|_D\le C_0r\}$, $r\ge1$, lie in one compact coordinate set. If $z_r,z'_r$ belong to a fixed compact coordinate set and their coordinate difference tends to zero, then $$\label{eq:d-scaling-equicontinuity}
 \mathop{\mathrm{dist}}_D(\Delta_r z_r,\Delta_r z'_r)=o(r).$$ For $v\in V_1$ one also has $$\label{eq:d-horizontal-flow-limit}
 \Delta_r^{-1}\bigl(\Delta_r z\exp_D(rt v)\bigr)
     \longrightarrow z\exp_\infty(tv),$$ uniformly when $z,t,v$ range over fixed compact sets. Haar measures on $D$ and $D_\infty$ are Lebesgue measure in these exponential coordinates, up to fixed scalars. If both scalars are chosen equal and $Q=\sum_jj\dim V_j$, then $\Delta_r$ multiplies Haar volume by $r^Q$, and $$\label{eq:d-polynomial-volume}
 c t^Q\le \mathop{\mathrm{vol}}_D\{d:|d|_D\le t\}\le C t^Q \qquad(t\ge1)$$ for some $c>0$. In particular $D$ has doubling Haar measure: $$\label{eq:nilpotent-doubling}
 \mathop{\mathrm{vol}}_D B_D(1,2t)\le C\mathop{\mathrm{vol}}_D B_D(1,t)\qquad(t>0).$$ Every conclusion of this lemma holds for an arbitrary connected, simply connected nilpotent Lie group $L$ equipped with a left invariant Riemannian metric and a splitting of its lower central filtration, with $L$ in place of $D$ and constants allowed to depend on $L$.

Each generator weight $\alpha_i$ vanishes on $[\mathfrak d,\mathfrak d]+(\mathfrak d\cap\mathfrak n)$, and inclusion of $\mathfrak d$ into $\mathfrak g$ induces an isomorphism $$\label{eq:canonical-cartan-height}
 W\cong V\Big/\bigcap_{i=1}^\nu\ker\alpha_i,
 \qquad V=\mathfrak d/[\mathfrak d,\mathfrak d].$$ Under this identification, $\pi$ kills $N$ and its restriction to $D$ is the displayed quotient of its abelianization. When $\nu=0$, the intersection on the right is all of $V$, and $W=0$; this occurs exactly when $G$ is nilpotent.*


*Write $\mathfrak n_\gamma=\mathfrak n\cap\mathfrak g_\gamma$ and view every weight occurring in $\mathfrak n$ as a linear form on $W$. For fixed norms, there are constants $C,M$ such that, for every $d\in D$ and every such weight $\gamma$, $$\label{eq:d-polynomial-unipotent}
 \mathop{\mathrm{Ad}}_d|_{\mathfrak n_\gamma}
     =e^{\gamma(\pi d)}U_\gamma(d),\qquad
 \|U_\gamma(d)\|+\|U_\gamma(d)^{-1}\|
     \le C(1+|d|_D)^M,$$ where $U_\gamma(d)$ is unipotent. For $w\in W$, the linear map $$\label{eq:exact-weight-dilation}
 S_w|_{\mathfrak n_\gamma}=e^{\gamma(w)}\mathop{\mathrm{id}}$$ is a Lie algebra automorphism and hence a group automorphism of $N$. Both $S_w$ and conjugation by $d$ preserve Haar measure on $N$. Consequently product Haar measure $dn\,dd$ is Haar measure on $N\rtimes D$.*


*The height map on $D_\infty$ is the homomorphism induced by $V_1\cong V\to W$, and it satisfies $$\label{eq:d-height-scaling}
 \pi(\Delta_r z)=r\pi(z)
 \qquad(z\in D_\infty, r>0).$$

The function $P$ is translation invariant, depends only on the convex hull, and is Lipschitz for Hausdorff distance. The function $$p(w)=P([0,w])$$ is a polyhedral norm on $W$. The group $$\label{eq:support-symmetry-group}
 \mathcal A=\{A\in\mathop{\mathrm{GL}}(W):P(AE)=P(E)\text{ for every nonempty
 compact convex }E\subset W\}$$ is finite. Its pullback action permutes the oriented rays of the generator weights $\alpha_i$.

Fix integers $a\ge b\ge1$ and $D\ge1$. There are constants $C>0$ and $A_1,A_2>0$, depending only on $a,b,D$, with the following property. Let $\Phi:\mathbb R^a\to\mathbb R^b$ be a polynomial map of degree at most $D$, all of whose coefficients have absolute value at most $B\ge0$. Suppose that one $b$-column minor of $D\Phi$ has a coefficient of absolute value at least $c>0$. Put $c_0=\min\{c,1\}$. For every $R\ge1$ and every Lebesgue-measurable $E\subset[-R,R]^a$ of measure $m>0$, $$\label{eq:polynomial-image-quantitative}
 |\Phi(E)|_{\mathrm{in}}
 \ge C c_0^{b+1}(1+B)^{-b^2}
          m^{A_1}(1+R)^{-A_2}.$$ One may take, with $d=\max\{1,b(D-1)\}$, $$A_1=1+ad(b+1),\qquad
 A_2=a+a^2d(b+1)+Db^2.$$*

*In particular, let $r\to\infty$ along a sequence and let $\Phi_r$ have these fixed dimensions and degree bound. Suppose their coefficients converge to those of a polynomial map which is submersive at some point. If the $E_r$ are Lebesgue-measurable and $$E_r\subset[-R_r,R_r]^a,\qquad
 1\le R_r\le e^{o(r)},\qquad |E_r|\ge e^{-o(r)},$$ then $$\label{eq:polynomial-image-subexponential}
 |\Phi_r(E_r)|_{\mathrm{in}}\ge e^{-o(r)}.$$ The same conclusion follows from <a href="#eq:polynomial-image-quantitative" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:polynomial-image-quantitative">[eq:polynomial-image-quantitative]</a> if the coefficient upper bounds $B_r$ and a chosen minor-coefficient lower bound $c_r$ satisfy $\log(1+B_r)+\log(1/\min\{c_r,1\})=o(r)$.

Let $J$ be a second countable unimodular locally compact group with Haar measure $\mu$. Let $Q\subset J$ be compact with $0<\mu(Q)<\infty$, and let $S\subset Q$ be Borel with $\mu(S)>0$. Fix an integer $h\ge1$ and compactly supported probability measures $\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_h$ on $J$. Suppose that numbers $D_j\ge1$ satisfy $$\mu(Q\mathop{\mathrm{supp}}\sigma_j)\le D_j\mu(Q)
       \qquad(1\le j\le h).$$ There are nested Borel sets $$S_0\subset S_1\subset\cdots\subset S_h=S$$ with positive measure such that, writing $a_j=\mu(S_j)/\mu(Q)$ and $\tau_j=a_j/(2D_j)$, one has $$\label{eq:recurrence-mass}
 a_{j-1}\ge\frac{a_j^2}{2D_j}$$ and $$\label{eq:recurrence-transition}
 \mathbb P\{x u_j\in S_j\}\ge\tau_j
       \qquad(x\in S_{j-1}),$$ where $u_j$ has law $\sigma_j*\check\sigma_j$. If the $u_j$ are sampled independently, every $x\in S_0$ therefore has probability at least $\prod_{j=1}^h\tau_j$ of satisfying $$x u_1\cdots u_j\in S_j\subset S
       \qquad(1\le j\le h).$$ In a sequence indexed by $r\to\infty$, if $h$ is fixed, $\mu(S)/\mu(Q)\ge e^{-o(r)}$, and every $D_j\le e^{o(r)}$, then $\mu(S_0)/\mu(Q)$ and the displayed success probability are at least $e^{-o(r)}$.

Let $\mu$ be an invariant ergodic probability on $X$. There is a continuous homomorphism $l:G\to W$ and functions $\phi_j\in L^2(X,\mu;W)$ such that, for every compact $Q\subset G$, $$\label{eq:compact-coboundaries}
 \sup_{x\in Q}
 \bigl\|b(x)+\phi_j\circ T_x-\phi_j-l(x)\bigr\|_{L^2(\mu)}
 \longrightarrow0.$$ The homomorphism $l$ kills $N$ and factors through $V$ on $D$. For each fixed $0<R<\infty$, $$\label{eq:stationary-sublinear}
 \lim_{r\to\infty}\frac1r\sup_{|x|_G\le Rr}
 \|\pi F(x)-l(x)\|_{L^2(\mu)}=0.$$ These assertions concern the fixed measure $\mu$; no rate uniform over invariant measures is asserted.

Let $\mu$ be an invariant probability on $X$ and let $A\in\mathop{\mathrm{GL}}(W)$. Suppose there are $\phi_j\in L^2(X,\mu;W)$ satisfying <a href="#eq:compact-coboundaries" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:compact-coboundaries">[eq:compact-coboundaries]</a> with $l=A\pi$. There is a finite nonzero $U$-invariant measure $\nu$ on $Y$ and functions $\theta_j\in L^2(Y,\nu;W)$ such that, for every compact $Q\subset G$, $$\label{eq:inverse-coboundaries}
 \sup_{y\in Q}
 \bigl\|\pi\bar F(y)+\theta_j\circ U_y-\theta_j-A^{-1}\pi y
 \bigr\|_{L^2(\nu)}\longrightarrow0.$$ In particular, for every fixed $0<R<\infty$, $$\label{eq:inverse-stationary-sublinear}
 \lim_{r\to\infty}\frac1r\sup_{|y|_G\le Rr}
 \|\pi\bar F(y)-A^{-1}\pi y\|_{L^2(\nu)}=0.$$

For every invariant ergodic probability $\mu$ on $X$, there is an $A_\mu\in\mathcal A$ such that <a href="#eq:compact-coboundaries" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:compact-coboundaries">[eq:compact-coboundaries]</a> holds with $l=A_\mu\pi$ and $$\lim_{r\to\infty}\frac1r\sup_{|x|_G\le Rr}
 \|\pi F(x)-A_\mu\pi x\|_{L^2(\mu)}=0
 \qquad(0<R<\infty).$$ The coupled finite invariant measure on $Y$ satisfies the analogous estimate with $A_\mu^{-1}$.

Let $\nu$ be a weak limit of chart-profile laws, and let $f\in\mathop{\mathrm{supp}}\nu$. If $$J=\{z\exp_\infty(tv):0\le t\le T\}\Subset\mathcal U_\delta,
 \qquad T>0,\quad v\in V,$$ then $$\label{eq:profile-convex-increments}
 \frac{f(z\exp_\infty(Tv))-f(z)}{T}
       \in\operatorname{conv}\{A\pi v:A\in\mathcal A\}.$$

Let $f:\mathcal U_\delta\to W$ be continuous and locally Lipschitz along horizontal segments, with locally uniform bounds for unit horizontal velocities. Suppose that, for every segment $J=\{z\exp_\infty(tv):0\le t\le T\}\Subset\mathcal U_\delta$, $T>0$, both $$\label{eq:affinity-two-inequalities}
 \frac{f(z\exp_\infty(Tv))-f(z)}{T}
       \in\operatorname{conv}(\mathcal A\pi v),
 \qquad P(f(J))\ge T p(\pi v)$$ hold. Then there exist $A\in\mathcal A$ and $b\in W$ such that $f(z)=A\pi z+b$ for all $z\in\mathcal U_\delta$.

For every fixed $\delta$ with $0<c\delta<1/2$ and every sequence in Definition <a href="#def:chart-profiles" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="def:chart-profiles">28</a>, the profile laws are relatively compact. Every function in the support of every limiting law has the form $$\label{eq:affine-profile-limit}
 f(z)=A\pi z+b\quad(z\in\mathcal U_\delta),
 \qquad A\in\mathcal A,\quad b\in W.$$

Fix $\delta$ as above, a function $\varepsilon(r)\ge0$ tending to zero, a nonempty compact set $Z\subset\mathcal U_\delta$, and $\eta>0$. Uniformly over the fixed family of map pairs, all $x\in G$, and all deterministic $k_0\in B_r((1+\delta)\mathcal C,e^{r\varepsilon(r)})$, $$\label{eq:distance-to-affine-class}
 \mathbb P_a\left\{
  \min_{A\in\mathcal A}\inf_{b\in W}
     \sup_{z\in Z}\|f_{r,x,ak_0}(z)-A\pi z-b\|\ge\eta
                 \right\}\longrightarrow0.$$ The conclusion also holds when $k_0$ is sampled independently of $a$ from any probability supported in the same allowed suffix set. In that case every supported limiting profile is still affine on $\mathcal U_\delta$.

Let $F$ belong to the fixed Lipschitz quasi-isometry family, let $A=A_F$, and let $\beta$ be a selected target ray representative. Put $\alpha=\beta\circ A$. There is a homeomorphism $$f_\beta:L_\alpha\longrightarrow L_\beta$$ and a fixed compact set $Q_\beta\subset L_\beta$ such that $$\label{eq:boundary-shadow}
 f_\beta(p_\alpha x)\in F(x)\cdot Q_\beta
 \qquad(x\in G).$$ The compact sets may be chosen uniformly over the fixed family. The map obtained from $\bar F$ for the reversed ray pair is $f_\beta^{-1}$.

For each source and target ray pair in Lemma <a href="#lem:ray-boundaries" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:ray-boundaries">45</a>, there is a fixed compact ball $U\subset L_\alpha$ with nonempty interior such that $$\label{eq:boundary-volume}
 \mathop{\mathrm{vol}}_{L_\beta}\bigl(f_\beta(x\cdot U)\bigr)
 \asymp e^{\ell_\beta(\pi F(x))}
 \qquad(x\in G).$$ The comparison constants and the choices of balls are uniform over the fixed quasi-isometry family.
